Operations
==========

Requirements
------------

Node 20 or newer, the Expo CLI via npx, and a physical Android or iOS device
for anything involving push notifications, the pedometer or location. A
simulator is fine for layout work and nothing else.

The backend must be running. This app has no mock layer; the only fallback is
the static activity catalogue in src/data/activityData.js, which covers the
log screen if the catalogue endpoint fails.


Environment
-----------

Create .env in the repository root.

	EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.20:8000/api

Rules for that value:

* It must end in /api. The backend mounts every route under it.
* It must be reachable from the device. On a phone, localhost is the phone.
  Use your machine's LAN address, or start Expo with --tunnel.
* The socket origin is derived from it by stripping the trailing /api, so one
  variable configures both REST and realtime.

Only variables prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ reach client code, and everything with
that prefix ships in the bundle. Never put a secret in this file.

The backend's CLIENT_ORIGINS must include the origin the app connects from or
every request fails before it reaches a route.


Running
-------

	npm install
	npx expo start
	npx expo start --android
	npx expo start --ios
	npx expo start --tunnel      device on a different network
	npx expo start -c            clear the Metro cache

Clear the cache after changing anything in src/data/avatarSources.js or
src/data/facultyBadgeSources.js. Metro resolves those require() paths at
bundle time and caches the result.


Configuration in app.json
-------------------------

	expo-notifications plugin, gold #FFD157 notification colour
	POST_NOTIFICATIONS permission on Android
	remote-notification background mode on iOS
	expo-location and expo-sensors permission strings

Changing any of these requires a new development build, not a reload. They are
native configuration.


Device permissions
------------------

	Notifications   asked for on first authenticated launch by
	                hooks/usePushNotifications.js
	Location        asked for by hooks/useGymProximity.js, used only for the
	                TLC proximity check
	Motion          asked for by hooks/usePedometer.js for step counts

All three fail quietly. A refused permission degrades the feature and must
never block a render or crash a screen.


First run against a fresh backend
---------------------------------

1. Start Postgres and the backend, and confirm it prints the REST base URL.
2. Register a student in the app. Faculty and nationality are required and
   determine the avatar set and leaderboard identity.
3. Retrieve the verification code from the development mail catcher the
   backend is pointed at.
4. Sign in, log an activity, and confirm XP moves on the home card.
5. Create a challenge in the admin console and confirm the push notification
   and the in-app banner both arrive.


Testing the pieces that need a device
-------------------------------------

	Push          physical device, signed in. The token is registered on
	              launch and removed on sign out. If nothing arrives, check
	              that POST /push/register succeeded before blaming the sender.
	Check-in      useGymProximity fires on a rising edge into the radius. To
	              test without walking to the TLC, temporarily widen the
	              radius rather than faking a location.
	Steps         needs motion permission and real movement.


Building
--------

Expo SDK 54. A production build goes through EAS. Before any build:

* Point EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL at the deployed API, not a LAN address. It is
  baked in at build time.
* Confirm the API is served over HTTPS. Access tokens are bearer credentials.
* Bump the version in app.json.

A development build is required for push and sensors. Expo Go cannot host
them.


Troubleshooting
---------------

	Requests fail immediately, login screen looks fine
	    EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL missing /api, or unreachable from the device.

	Login works, nothing updates live
	    The socket origin is wrong, or the access token expired between the
	    REST call and the handshake. Both use the same token.

	Text renders in the system font
	    A font family string in src/theme/typography.js does not match a
	    useFonts key in App.js.

	An avatar or crest is blank
	    A require() path in avatarSources.js or facultyBadgeSources.js does
	    not resolve. Clear the Metro cache and check the file exists.

	Everyone on a board looks identical
	    facultyKey and level were flattened somewhere between the API
	    response and the board. Carry the serializer fields through.